s told every day of my life that I  didn't look Jewish for example every day  as a child she was the little blond  Jewish model who Kodak featured in a  Christmas ad I would be like yea raised  kosher went to yeshiva fluent in Hebrew  I was almost like militant about it the  other clue came from her mother who let  slip when Danny was 25 that she'd been  conceived at a fertility clinic in  Philadelphia and I said what was the  procedure and she said artificial  insemination she asked if a sperm donor  was involved she said absolutely not can  you imagine such a thing it would mean  that your father wouldn't have known  that his child was Jewish so you tucked  it wide closed the door yeah and it  didn't open again for decades until  ancestry.com gave her a different answer  when I started to come to the conclusion  that my parents had known what they were  doing there was such a sense of betrayal  that they kept my identity from me do  you understand why they did what they  did I had to go inside of what it must  have been like to be them in the early  1960s and there was so much shame  surrounding infertility especially  Malins infertility  the other thing you wrestle with  obviously in this circumstance is is my  father still my father I mean the irony  for me is that my mother who was my  biological parent is not someone that I  ever felt bonded to my father who it  turns out was not my biological parent  is someone with whom I have a profound  bond you find your biological father  from Markab Lee quickly 36 hours and the  time that I made the discovery about my  dad well until the moment that I was  looking at a YouTube video of my  biological father  Shapiro protects his privacy in  inheritance but reveals he was a 78 year  old retired doctor who'd been a sperm  donor was it easier for you to deal with  this because you could write about it  writing is how I process everything it's  kind of my church it's my it's my faith  it's my temple it's it's how I  understand the world inheritance is a  meditation on identity by a woman whose  own sense of self was shaken to the core  what did you learn about identity that  it comes from so many different places a  friend of mine early on said to me when  you get to the other side of this you  will be free do you feel free I do if  you're free what are you free of there  was always some kind of disconnect  within me something that I felt a little  haunted by that feels going that really  feels gone we create a mythology for  ourselves that we carry with us so when  were forced to rewrite it it's very  upsetting right but to understand it as  mythology is actually pretty liberating  one thing that made this difficult for  Danny was her parents were both long  deceased her biological father actually  went on become a medical ethicist but  even he said when he was a student and a  sperm donor he had never considered you  know that he might end up having kids  and this is the big question of this  book which is should children have the  right to know what their origins are Wow  this so resonates with me and you know  why yes you know why

